<methodology>
Backward taint analysis from redirect sinks.
1. Sinks. redirect()/res.redirect(), setting the Location header, 30x responses, meta-refresh
   or JS `location =` emitted server-side. Trace the destination backward to its source.
2. Sources. next/return/returnUrl/redirect/callback/url params in query, body, or headers.
3. Adequate control. An allowlist of permitted hosts (or strictly-relative paths that also
   reject protocol-relative URLs) is the only sufficient defense.
4. Bypass checks. Protocol-relative `//evil.com`, backslashes `/\evil.com`, embedded
   credentials `https://trusted@evil.com`, whitespace/control chars, and `startswith("/")`
   defeated by `//`.
5. Sanitizer context-match. A host check counts only if it parses the URL the same way the
   redirect does and runs before the sink with no later mutation.
</methodology>

<examples>
Illustrative vulnerable patterns (different languages; NOT specific to this repo —
do not assume these files exist here; use them to recognise the shape of the bug):
  - python: `return redirect(request.args["next"])`
    why: the next param becomes the Location with no host allowlist -> redirect to any site.
  - javascript: `res.redirect(req.query.url)`
    why: user url is redirected to verbatim; `//evil.com` is treated as an absolute external URL.
  - python: `if url.startswith("/"): return redirect(url)`
    why: the `/`-prefix check is bypassed by `//evil.com`, which browsers treat as protocol-relative.
</examples>

<false_positives_to_avoid>
- A redirect to a fixed, server-chosen URL is not a finding.
- A check that the target begins with "/" AND explicitly rejects "//" and "/\" is adequate.
- Reflected open redirect is still a finding even if it "only" enables phishing/OAuth theft.
</false_positives_to_avoid>

Confidence: High = clear unprotected path, deterministic. Medium = strongly
indicated but a material uncertainty remains. Low = plausible but the trace is
incomplete. Rule: when uncertain, round down (favour Medium/Low).

Begin by reading the entry-point files above (or, if none, list_dir the scope),
then trace each candidate toward the sinks above.

